Recommended implementation roadmap
Do not develop all three agents simultaneously. Build them in stages and test each before proceeding.
Phase 1 — AI foundation
Start here

- Set up Gemini API.
- Create central AI provider service.
- Implement agent configuration.
- Add API usage tracking and rate limiting.
- Create basic AI administration settings.

Phase 2 — Physiotherapy Assistant
- Build English and Kiswahili chat.
- Integrate verified physiotherapy knowledge.
- Implement safety and emergency escalation.
- Save conversation history.
- Add feedback and reporting.

Phase 3 — Forum Content Agent
- Retrieve forum subcategories.
- Generate daily educational topics.
- Detect duplicate topics.
- Implement admin email verification.
- Publish approved topics automatically.

Phase 4 — Virtual Administrator
- Connect application statistics.
- Monitor application activity.
- Generate daily administrative reports.
- Identify suspicious activities.
- Introduce controlled administrative recommendations.

Phase 5 — Optimization
- Introduce provider fallback.
- Optimize token consumption.
- Add agent performance monitoring.
- Evaluate paid API options if necessary.


Important considerations before production
There are four things I would not compromise on:
1. Patient privacy: Avoid transmitting identifiable patient information to third-party AI APIs. Establish data minimization, retention and consent policies.
2. Medical accuracy: Build a verified knowledge base reviewed by qualified physiotherapists. AI-generated answers must not be treated as clinically validated.
3. Security: Never expose API keys in React. Keep all AI requests on the backend.
4. Human authority: AI agents must never become independent administrators with unrestricted access to your MongoDB database.
For the physiotherapy assistant, I would additionally implement a safety classifier before the main AI response. This can identify emergency symptoms and potentially dangerous requests before educational guidance is generated.